      "text": "1. Let the four consecutive positive integers be \\( n, n+1, n+2, n+3 \\). We are given that their product is 840, i.e.,\n\n\\[\nn(n+1)(n+2)(n+3) = 840\n\\]\n\n2. Start by approximating \\( n \\) and then attempting to solve the inequality \\( n(n+3) \\leqslant 840 \\):\n\n\\[\nn^2 + 3n \\leqslant 840\n\\]\n\n3. By solving the quadratic equation \\( n^2 + 3n - 840 = 0 \\):\n\n\\[\nn = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-840)}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{7849}}{2}\n\\]\n\n4. Approximate the solution where \\( n^2 + 3n - 840 = 0 \\):\n\n\\[\nn = \\frac{-3 \\pm 89}{2} \n\\]\n\n5. The positive integer \\( n \\) is approximately \\( n = 44 \\) from the sum:\n\n\\[\n44, 45, 46, 47\n\\]\n\n6. We need to check whether these integers satisfy:\n\n\\[\n44 \\cdot 45 \\cdot 46 \\cdot 47 < 840\n\\]\n\n7. If so, \\( n = 44 \\) is too small; if not, \\( n = 45 \\):\n\n\\[\n40 \\cdot 41 \\cdot 42 \\cdot 43 < 840\n\\]\n\n8. Note:\n\n\\[\n40 \\cdot 41 \\cdot 42 = 68080 > 840\n\\]\n\n\\[\n41 \\cdot 42 \\cdot 43 = 78312 > 840\n\\]\n\n9. \\( 45 \\cdot 46 \\cdot 47 = 99560 > 840 \\).\n\n10. Since \\( 45 \\cdot 46 \\cdot 47 \\) is too large, \\( n = 45 \\) is invalid; thus \\( n = 44 \\) is too small\n\n11. Recheck smaller possible valid integers by incrementing \\( n \\) by 1 and finding correct values.\n\nAfter confirming with calculations:\n\nConsecutive integer problem solved finding \\(\\boxed{44}\\) as 45 exceeds 840 and immediately yielding 44 gives the right target. Smaller sums before reach 840 diminishing solution.",
